    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_1e256ae1b852a25ce791f8c48f39f4ea58353c5cd66b827273410ba12cd40b44",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The analytic-toolkit unit turns the squarefree discriminant average into a Gauss-weighted dual-frequency sum. The main-term extraction unit then separates k=0, where the Gauss sum enforces a square condition and yields the primary residues, from k≠0, where poles occur precisely in the square-frequency case and force a secondary principal term. The cancellation unit factors and continues that secondary series, uses an even functional identity to evaluate it, and recombines it with the zero-frequency term so that the ±δ residues cancel while the ±τ terms survive; averaged bilinear estimates control the remaining frequencies. The final main term therefore depends on the interaction of the zero and square nonzero sectors and is not available from any parent unit alone.",
